{
  "dom_id": "step-q215423",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The point of intersection of the lines $x-y=7$ and $3x+2y=11$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-2y$ term that can cancel with the $2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=7 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ 3x+2y=11 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=14 \\\\ 3x+2y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ (2x+3x) & =(14+11) \\\\ 5x & =25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=5$ into the equation $x-y=7$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =7 \\\\ 5-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=5,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(5,-2).$",
